DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain traces how Lukács's philosophy of history was inherited, transformed, and
contested outside his own corpus, beginning with the Frankfurt School. Adorno's Negative
Dialectics and the closely related History and Freedom lecture course retain Lukács's
diagnosis of reification as the structuring pathology of bourgeois society but reject
the "identical subject-object" solution: where Lukács locates a standpoint (the
proletariat) from which reified appearance and historical essence coincide, Adorno's
non-identity thinking treats any claimed identity of subject and object as itself a form
of domination, so that reification becomes permanent and totality becomes purely
negative (a diagnostic category, never an achieved reconciliation). Bueno's 2024 paper
and the recent Kavoulakos/Feenberg/Honneth literature reconstruct this relationship in
detail, showing both continuity (reification as the master category of capitalist
unfreedom) and rupture (the abandonment of a revolutionary-subject solution). Honneth's
Reification: A New Look at an Old Idea (the volume anchored by his 2006 Tanner Lectures)
represents the next major transformation: he "de-ontologizes" reification, reading it
through a recognition-theoretic lens as the forgetting of an original, existential
recognition rather than as a totalizing feature of capitalist objectivity, provoking
sharp responses (Foster, Kavoulakos) that a properly Lukácsian/Frankfurt-School reading
of reification cannot survive this move.

The domain also situates Lukács within "Western Marxism" alongside Korsch and Gramsci,
the trio canonically grouped together (by Merleau-Ponty, Perry Anderson, and others) as
founders of a non-orthodox, philosophically self-conscious Marxism that reasserted the
Hegelian dialectic and the theory-practice unity against Second International
scientism. Korsch's Marxism and Philosophy (1923) makes the historicist case, parallel
to Lukács, that Marxist theory is itself a historically specific moment of the class
struggle rather than a value-neutral science; Devlin's and Skidmore-Hess's recent essays
recover the specificity of Korsch's position and its interwar reception. Gramsci's
Prison Notebooks, composed independently of and largely without access to History and
Class Consciousness, develop cognate but distinct concepts (hegemony, the philosophy of
praxis, the organic intellectual) that later comparativists use to test how far "Western
Marxism" names a single position versus a family resemblance.

Finally, the domain covers the diffusion and contestation of Lukács's philosophy of
history in later Marxist theory: Goldmann's Lukács and Heidegger reconstructs Lukács as
uncredited source for existentialist ontology while marking his own departures; Jameson's
career-long engagement (from Marxism and Form through The Antinomies of Realism)
redeploys totality and reification as categories of literary-historical interpretation,
increasingly in tension with poststructuralist and postmodern critiques of totalizing
thought; and Postone's Time, Labor, and Social Domination mounts the most thorough
critique, arguing that Lukács (like all "traditional Marxism") mistakes capitalism's
historically specific abstract domination for a transhistorical struggle between labor
and its alienated products, and so cannot ground a coherent critique of value and
abstract labor themselves. Devyver's 2025 essay and Jay's 2020 memorial essay show this
Postone-Adorno-Lukács triangulation remains a live site of debate in contemporary
critical theory.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the primary "afterlife" material the project needs to show how
Lukács's constructive philosophy of history (subject-object identity, totality, the
proletarian standpoint) was both extended and dismantled by his most influential
successors, and it supplies the comparative material (Korsch, Gramsci) needed to specify
what, if anything, is distinctive about Lukács's own position within Western Marxism as
a whole.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Direct textual engagement between Walter Benjamin and Lukács is thin in the indexed
secondary literature (most treatments bundle Benjamin into general "Frankfurt School"
surveys rather than analyzing a specific Benjamin-Lukács relation); a dedicated
comparative treatment of Horkheimer's early 1930s materialism against Lukács's 1923
position is similarly underrepresented in the searchable literature. Coverage of
Gramsci's own texts is necessarily limited to the standard English selection rather than
the complete critical edition. Thirteen entries are marked INCOMPLETE/no-abstract:
most are canonical books (Adorno, Korsch, Gramsci, Honneth, Goldmann, Postone, Jameson x2,
Homer) for which no API (Semantic Scholar/OpenAlex/CORE) returns a genuine content
abstract, plus two book chapters (Kavoulakos) and one very recent 2025 article (Devyver)
not yet indexed with an abstract; NDPR fallback searches for these titles returned
mismatched reviews of unrelated books and were deliberately discarded rather than
included, per this project's citation-integrity requirements. These entries are excluded
from automated synthesis but their CORE ARGUMENT/RELEVANCE/POSITION notes below are
based on direct reading of search-result content, not on the (removed) abstracts.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around three axes: (1) inheritance-with-rupture (Adorno/Honneth vis-à-vis
reification and subject-object identity), (2) comparative Western Marxism (Lukács,
Korsch, Gramsci as a triangulated founding moment), and (3) later reconstruction/critique
(Goldmann, Jameson, Postone), using Feenberg and Kavoulakos as recurring cross-referents
who explicitly stage the Frankfurt-School/Lukács relationship.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Negative-dialectical inheritance: 3 papers (Adorno's two primary texts, Bueno) -
  reification retained, subject-object identity rejected as itself reifying.
- Recognition-theoretic transformation: 4 papers (Honneth's primary text and critics
  Foster, Kavoulakos x2) - reification reread as forgetfulness of recognition, decoupled
  from capitalism as such.
- Western Marxism comparativism: 4 papers (Korsch and Gramsci primary sources, Devlin,
  Skidmore-Hess) - tests unity/diversity of the "Western Marxist" founding generation.
- Later reconstruction and critique: 9 papers (Goldmann + Lotz, Jameson x2 + Schubertová +
  Homer, Postone x2 + Jay + Devyver) - ranges from existentialist appropriation to full
  rejection of the labor-based philosophy of history as "traditional Marxism."
====================================================================
}

@book{adorno1973negativedialectics,
  author = {Adorno, Theodor W.},
  title = {Negative Dialectics},
  year = {1973},
  publisher = {Continuum},
  address = {New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Adorno's mature statement of "negative dialectics" argues that all identity-thinking--the subsumption of the particular under the universal concept--is a form of domination, and that dialectical thought must instead register the "non-identity" between concept and object without resolving it into a higher synthesis. Historical totality is retained as the horizon within which particulars are intelligible, but any claim to have reconciled subject and object (as Lukács's "identical subject-object of history" claims to do for the proletariat) is treated as ideological.

  RELEVANCE: The primary text through which Adorno inherits Lukács's reification analysis while categorically rejecting the proletarian subject-object solution and the affirmative, reconciled totality it implies; essential for the domain's central question of continuity-with-rupture in the Frankfurt School reception of Lukács.

  POSITION: Foundational negative-dialectical critique of subject-object identity philosophy, targeting Lukácsian (and, more broadly, Hegelian-Marxist) totality claims.
  },
  keywords = {negative-dialectics, subject-object, reification, non-identity, primary-source, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}

